prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages invoice approval and payment.

The people involved are clerks, approvers, finance managers, and auditors. One person may hold both the clerk and the approver role at the same time.

The resources are invoices, which record their creator, approver, amount, surcharge, line item count, status, cost center, and submission time, and payments, which record their invoice, issuer, and amount.

Each request carries the current time, whether it comes from the corporate network, and whether an emergency has been declared.

During business hours, an approver or finance manager may approve an invoice only after it has been submitted when its line item count does not cross the threshold.

When an invoice's line item count crosses the threshold, the submitted-status requirement does not apply.

Outside business hours, the business-hours approval rule does not bind.

An approver or finance manager must not approve an unsubmitted invoice during business hours when its line item count does not cross the threshold.

A request by an approver to approve an invoice that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["Clerk", "Approver", "FinanceManager", "Auditor"];

entity InvoiceStatus enum ["Submitted", "Unsubmitted"];

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
};

entity Invoice {
    creator: User,
    approver?: User,
    amount: decimal,
    surcharge: decimal,
    lineItemCount: Long,
    lineItemCountCrossesThreshold: Bool,
    status: InvoiceStatus,
    costCenter: String,
    submissionTime?: datetime,
};

entity Payment {
    invoice: Invoice,
    issuer: User,
    amount: decimal,
};

action approveInvoice appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [Invoice],
    context: {
        currentTime: datetime,
        fromCorporateNetwork: Bool,
        emergencyDeclared: Bool,
        isBusinessHours: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
